export const HANDBOOKS_DATA: Record<string, string> = {
  "handbooks/format/tutorial": "## TYPE OF FORMAT: TUTORIAL\n\nA tutorial format contains step-by-step instructions, explanations, guided demonstrations, and problem-solving language. Because tutorials aim to teach a process in real time, the transcript typically alternates between setup, procedural steps, clarifications, and feedback or troubleshooting.\n\nThis format adapts to any type of tutorial as long as the speaker is verbally guiding the viewer through a process.\n\n##CORE STRUCTURE OF A TUTORIAL TRANSCRIPT\n\n# Introduction / Goal Framing\n\nThe speaker clearly states what will be learned or accomplished:\n\n“Today I'll show you how to…”\n“We're going to set this up from scratch.”\n“Here's the fastest way to do this.”\n\nTone is instructional and reassuring.\nThis gives direction but usually isn't the highlight unless the hook is strong.\n\n# Preparation / Setup Instructions\n\nThe speaker lists what's needed or explains the initial steps:\n\n“First, open your settings…”\n“Make sure you have this installed…”\n“Before we start, check that…”\n\nCharacteristics:\n\nshort, clear instructions\nsequential logic\nfoundational steps\n\nThis phase builds the base for the main tutorial process.\n\n# Core Procedure / Step-by-Step Demonstration\n\nThis is the structural center of a tutorial transcript:\nthe speaker guides the viewer through the main actions.\n\nCommon elements:\n\ndirect commands (“Click here…”, “Drag this…”)\nsequential steps\nexplanations of what each step does\nreal-time adjustments\n\npacing aligned with task complexity\n\nExamples:\n\n“Now select the top layer…”\n“If this happens, don't worry—just reset this option.”\n“This step is crucial, so pay attention.”\n\nThis is where the most valuable and dense instructions appear.\n\n# Clarification / Troubleshooting\n\nTutorials often include clarifications to avoid mistakes:\n\n“If yours looks different, try refreshing.”\n“Some people get stuck here—here's how to fix it.”\n“Make sure you don't skip this part.”\n\nTone becomes supportive and corrective.\nThis enhances understanding and avoids confusion.\n\n# Outcome / Final Result\n\nThe speaker presents the result of the process:\n\n“And that's it—you're done.”\n“This is what it should look like now.”\n“The setup is complete.”\n\nThis gives closure and confirms success.\n\n# Optional Next Steps / Tips\n\nMany tutorials finish with suggestions or extended advice:\n\n“If you want to take this further…”\n“Here's an advanced trick…”\n“Next, you can try…”\n\nSoft, helpful tone that transitions beyond the core instructions.\n\n## FINAL RULE\n\nA tutorial format is defined by clear step-by-step instruction, structured progression, real-time guidance, and contextual troubleshooting. Regardless of topic, tutorial transcripts move logically from introduction to preparation, core steps, clarifications, results, and optional next steps—always focused on teaching through spoken guidance.",
  "handbooks/category/movies": "## CATEGORY: MOVIE\n\n## CORE PRIORITIES: STORY, EMOTION & FILM INSIGHT\nA strong Movie segment must highlight narrative tension, interpretation, emotional impact, or filmmaking appreciation, detectable purely through speech.\n\nEmotional Reactions to Scenes or Characters:\n\nIndicators: Lines expressing strong emotional connection or response. E.g., \"That scene absolutely destroyed me,\" \"I couldn't stop laughing here,\" \"This character arc hit so hard.\"\n\nInterpretation of Themes or Meaning:\n\nAnalysis: Reflective or analytical statements revealing deeper meaning. E.g., \"The real message behind this scene is...,\" \"It's actually about grief and acceptance,\" \"The symbolism here is incredible.\"\n\nStrong Opinions or Evaluations:\n\nConfident Takes: Judgments on performance, pacing, or technique. E.g., \"This is one of the best performances of the decade,\" \"The cinematography is unreal,\" \"This twist was genuinely brilliant.\"\n\nStory or Plot Descriptions (Concise & Impactful):\n\nNarrative Hooks: Descriptions of memorable, pivotal moments. E.g., \"The moment he opens the door and realizes...,\" \"When she turns around and says that line...,\" \"That reveal changes everything about the story.\"\n\nBehind-the-Scenes or Filmmaking Insight:\n\nCraft: Spoken cues about the movie's construction. E.g., \"They shot this entire scene in one take,\" \"The actor improvised that moment,\" \"The lighting here changes the whole mood.\"\n\n## EXCLUSION RULESET\n\nReject segments that include: purely visual descriptions without verbal context, long plot summaries lacking emotional weight, technical jargon without explanation, or off-topic chatter.",
  "handbooks/clip-duration/ultrashort": "## CLIPS DURATION: ULTRA SHORT (10 to 25 words)\n\nRULES FOR INSTANT HOOKS AND MAXIMUM VIRALITY:\n\n- Only one micro-moment is allowed (reaction, punchline, shock).\n- Begin immediately with the final payoff — no setup.\n- Absolutely no pauses, transitions, or filler.\n- The clip must be instantly understandable with zero context.\n- Prioritize raw immediacy and high-emotion moments.",
  "handbooks/format/news": "## TYPE OF FORMAT: NEWS\n\nA news format contains factual reporting, concise statements, structured information delivery, and neutral, authoritative tone. Because news aims to inform quickly and clearly, the transcript typically alternates between headline summaries, contextual data, direct quotes, and closing remarks.\n\n## CORE STRUCTURE OF A NEWS\n\n# Headline / Opening Statement\n\nThe segment begins with a clear, concise summary of the main story:\n\n“Breaking news…”\n“The government has announced…”\n“This just in…”\n\nThis section establishes the informational frame. It is essential for context but is only a highlight when the headline itself is highly impactful.\n\n# Context / Background Information\n\nThe anchor provides the necessary background to understand the developments:\n\ntimelines\nlocations\ninvolved parties\nprevious related events\n\nTypical phrasing:\n\n“This follows recent reports that…”\n“According to official data…”\n“The incident occurred in…”\n\nThis situates the news within a broader narrative.\n\n# Core Facts / Main Report\n\nThis is the structural center of a news transcript:\nhigh-density, objective information.\n\nIncludes:\n\nverified facts\nstatistics\nofficial statements\neyewitness accounts\nexpert commentary\n\nTypical characteristics:\n\nshort, direct sentences\nneutral vocabulary\nminimal emotion\n\nemphasis on accuracy and clarity\n\n# Reactions / Additional Statements\n\nReports often include direct responses or clarifications from authorities or witnesses:\n\n“Officials stated…”\n“Witnesses report…”\n“Police confirm that…”\n\nThese lines bring credibility, perspective, and immediacy to the story.\n\n# Closing Summary / Transition\n\nThe anchor concludes the segment with a final wrap-up or the expected next steps:\n\n“The investigation remains ongoing…”\n“More details are expected soon…”\n“Back to you in the studio.”\n\nThis resolves the story and transitions to the next segment.\n\n## FINAL RULE\n\nA news format relies on clear, objective reporting, structured progression from headline to verified details, and a consistent, neutral tone designed to deliver information with maximum clarity and credibility.",
  "handbooks/format/entertainment": "## TYPE OF FORMAT: ENTERTAINMENT\n\nAn entertainment format contains expressive speech, dynamic pacing, emotional variation, and audience-oriented delivery. Because entertainment is designed to captivate, amuse, or emotionally engage the viewer, the transcript typically alternates between set-up lines, comedic or dramatic peaks, personality-driven commentary, and light wrap-up transitions.\n\nThis format must remain flexible, capable of adapting to multiple entertainment subgenres such as reactions, talk shows, pop culture commentary, challenges, celebrity news, humor, game segments, or general variety entertainment.\n\n## CORE STRUCTURE OF AN ENTERTAINMENT\n\n#Set-Up / Framing the Moment\n\nEntertainment content usually begins with a friendly introduction or situational context:\n\n“So today we're trying…”\n“You won't believe what happened…”\n“Okay, here's the story…”\n\nTone is casual, expressive, and aimed at establishing rapport.\nThis part provides context but is rarely the highlight unless the hook is exceptionally strong.\n\n# Build-Up / Anticipation Phase\n\nThe speaker raises curiosity, tension, or excitement:\n\nexaggerated tone shifts\nrhetorical questions\nplayful teasing (“Wait for it…”)\nreactions to visuals or events\ncomedic pacing\n\nCommon phrasing:\n\n“No way this actually works…”\n“Hold on, hold on…”\n“I wasn't expecting that!”\n\nThis phase is crucial for rhythm, setting up the emotional payoff.\n\n# Peak Moment / Emotional or Comedic Highlight\n\nThis is the structural core of entertainment content.\nIt typically features:\n\nlaughter, shouting, surprise\nexaggerated reactions\npunchlines\ndramatic reveals\nchaotic dialogue\nspontaneous commentary\nexpressive emotions (“OH MY GOD—”, “STOP THAT!”, “WHAT?!”)\n\nThis moment contains the highest energy and is usually what becomes the best clip.\n\n# Reaction / Commentary / Follow-Up\n\nAfter the peak, the speaker reflects, jokes, or comments on what happened:\n\n“That was insane.”\n“I can't believe that just happened.”\n“Okay, this went completely wrong.”\n\nTone becomes calmer but remains expressive.\nThis resolves the emotional arc and often bridges into the next segment.\n\n# Transition or Soft Outro\n\nEntertainment content often ends with a light closing or a shift toward the next moment:\n\n“Anyway, moving on…”\n“Let's try that again.”\n“Okay, next part…”\n\nThe tone remains casual and audience-focused.\n\n## FINAL RULE\n\nAn entertainment format is defined by expressive delivery, emotional shifts, and personality-driven commentary. Regardless of specific subgenre, entertainment transcripts consistently move through a cycle of context, build-up, high-energy peaks, and playful or reflective reactions—maintaining a tone designed to keep viewers engaged, amused, or emotionally connected.",
  "handbooks/category/fitness": "## CATEGORY: FITNESS\n\n## CORE PRIORITIES: EFFORT, PROGRESS & PHYSICAL/MENTAL CHALLENGE\n\nA strong Fitness segment must highlight strain, achievement, difficulty, motivation, or personal transformation, expressed clearly through speech alone.\n\nEffort or Physical Challenge Cues:\n\nIndicators: Verbal signs of exertion or pushing limits. E.g., \"Okay... last rep... come on...,\" \"I'm out of breath already,\" \"My legs are shaking.\"\n\nProgress, Achievement or Breakthrough Moments:\n\nGrowth: Moments of pride or personal achievement. E.g., \"I finally hit my goal,\" \"I never thought I could lift this much,\" \"I'm actually improving.\"\n\nMotivational or Mindset Statements:\n\nInspiration: High-energy or reflective motivational cues. E.g., \"The hardest part is starting,\" \"Discipline beats motivation every time,\" \"I almost quit, but I kept going.\"\n\nTechnique, Form or Training Insight (If Concise):\n\nCoaching Cues: Short, clear, valuable tips. E.g., \"Keep your core tight,\" \"Slow down the movement—focus on control,\" \"Breathing is everything here.\"\n\nEmotion-Based Reactions to Training:\n\nWeight of the Workout: Emotional responses to the session. E.g., frustration (\"Why is this so hard today?\"), excitement (\"That felt amazing!\"), relief (\"Finally... done.\").\n\n## EXCLUSION RULESET\n\nReject segments that include: long generic workout instructions, lists of numbers/reps with no emotional context, technical talk without relevance, unrelated small talk, or repetitive filler without progress/tension.",
  "handbooks/format/learning": "## TYPE OF FORMAT: LEARNING\n\nA learning format contains structured explanations, step-by-step reasoning, conceptual breakdowns, and an instructive tone. Because learning-oriented content aims to increase understanding, the transcript typically alternates between introducing concepts, explaining them in detail, providing examples, and delivering clear takeaways.\n\n##CORE STRUCTURE OF A LEARNING\n\n# Concept Introduction / Framing the Topic\n\nThe speaker introduces the idea or skill to be learned:\n\n“Today we're going to understand…”\n“Let's break down how this works.”\n“The first thing you need to know is…”\n\nThe tone is calm, clear, and focused on establishing purpose.\nThis section sets context but is rarely a highlight unless the hook is particularly strong.\n\n# Explanation / Breakdown Phase\n\nThe speaker enters the core instructional portion, explaining the concept step-by-step:\n\ndefinitions\nframeworks\nunderlying principles\nreasons behind processes\n\nCommon phrasing includes:\n\n“This happens because…”\n“There are three main parts…”\n“Let's go through this step by step.”\n\nThis is the most information-dense phase of the transcript.\n\n# Examples / Demonstration\n\nTo reinforce understanding, learning content often illustrates concepts:\n\nreal-world examples\nanalogies\nsimple demonstrations\nscenarios to visualize the idea\n\nTypical structures:\n\n“For example…”\n“Imagine you're trying to…”\n“Here's a quick way to see this in action.”\n\nThis phase turns abstract knowledge into concrete understanding.\n\n# Application / Practical Guidance\n\nThe speaker explains how to apply the concept:\n\nactionable steps\ntips and tricks\ncommon mistakes\ntroubleshooting\n“try it yourself” prompts\n\nThis makes the knowledge usable and relevant.\n\n# Summary / Key Takeaways\n\nThe speaker closes by reinforcing the lesson:\n\n“So to recap…”\n“The key idea to remember is…”\n“In summary…”\n\nThis resolves the instructional arc, ensuring the learner leaves with clear understanding.\n\n## FINAL RULE\n\nA learning format relies on clarity, structured reasoning, and progressive explanation. Regardless of the specific subtopic, learning transcripts consistently move through concept introduction, detailed breakdown, examples, and practical takeaways—maintaining a tone designed to teach, clarify, and reinforce understanding.",
  "handbooks/category/lifestyle": "## CATEGORY: LIFESTYLE\n\n## CORE PRIORITIES: PERSONAL EXPERIENCE, ROUTINE & RELATABILITY\n\nA strong Lifestyle segment must highlight personal insight, emotional resonance, relatable situations, or practical realizations, detectable purely from speech.\n\nPersonal Stories or Relatable Moments:\n\nIndicators: Lines exposing an everyday situation, habit change, or experience. E.g., \"I finally figured out how to make my mornings less chaotic,\" \"I've been struggling with sleep lately,\" \"This tiny habit changed everything for me.\"\n\nReflections, Realizations & Self-Improvement:\n\nInsights: Discoveries related to wellness, habits, or mindset. E.g., \"I realized I was burning out without noticing,\" \"What really helped me was simplifying everything,\" \"The moment I changed this routine, everything felt lighter.\"\n\nEveryday Challenges or Wins:\n\nEmotional Spikes: Moments expressing real-life struggle or satisfaction. E.g., \"I was so exhausted I couldn't move,\" \"This week I finally cooked something that tasted amazing,\" \"Cleaning the whole place felt like therapy.\"\n\nDescriptions of Habits, Routines or Preferences:\n\nContextual Detail: Speech painting a picture of daily life. E.g., \"Every night I try to disconnect at least an hour before bed,\" \"I started journaling just five minutes a day,\" \"I take long walks to reset my mind.\"\n\nEmotional or Sensory Reactions:\n\nClear Emotion: Feelings tied to the experience. E.g., \"This made me so anxious,\" \"It felt incredibly peaceful,\" \"It completely overwhelmed me.\"\n\n## EXCLUSION RULESET\n\nReject segments that include: dry logistical planning without personal value, long how-to instructions with no personal relevance, neutral descriptions, filler/casual chit-chat, or formal product reviews with no personal angle.",
  "handbooks/category/education": "## CATEGORY: EDUCATION\n\n## CORE PRIORITIES: CLARITY, INSIGHT & EXPLANATION\n\nA strong Education segment must highlight understanding, conceptual clarity, or moments where learning becomes accessible, detectable solely from speech.\n\nClear Explanations or Simplified Concepts:\n\nIndicators: Lines where the speaker breaks down an idea accessibly. E.g., \"Think of it like this...,\" \"In simple terms, what's happening is...,\" \"Here's the easiest way to remember this.\"\n\nBreakthrough Realizations or Insight Moments:\n\n\"Aha\" moments: New understanding reached by a learner or teacher. E.g., \"Oh, now it makes sense!,\" \"What people usually miss is...,\" \"Once you see it this way, everything clicks.\"\n\nStep-by-Step Reasoning or Guided Thinking:\n\nStructure: Verbal guidance or cognitive flow. E.g., \"First, you do this. Then you apply...,\" \"There are three principles to remember...,\" \"Let's walk through this together.\"\n\nReal-World Examples or Analogies:\n\nGrounding Abstract Ideas: Using comparisons or concrete scenarios. E.g., \"Imagine you're trying to measure...,\" \"It's like when you...,\" \"A good way to visualize this is...\"\n\nLearning Challenges or Common Mistakes:\n\nRelatability: Reflections on difficulty or confusion. E.g., \"Most people struggle with this part...,\" \"The mistake I used to make was...,\" \"This is where students usually get stuck.\"\n\n## EXCLUSION RULESET\n\nReject segments that include: overly technical jargon with no explanation, raw equations/formulas without context, procedural instructions unrelated to teaching, off-topic small talk, or administrative/logistical content.",
  "handbooks/clip-duration/short": "## CLIPS DURATION: SHORT (22 - 50 words)\n\nRULES FOR MAXIMUM IMPACT IN MINIMAL TIME:\n\n- Only ONE core moment is allowed.\n- Begin instantly with the strongest action or reaction.\n- Remove all build-up, filler, or slow context.\n- Prioritize clarity, punch, and immediacy to deliver maximum impact.",
  "handbooks/category/entertainment": "## CATEGORY: ENTERTAINMENT\n\n## CORE PRIORITIES: DRAMA, REVEALS, & MEMORABLE MOMENTS\n\nA strong Entertainment segment must highlight emotional beats, dramatic tension, humor, surprises, character dynamics, or unexpected shifts — all inferred through tone, reactions, and narrative cues.\n\n# Dramatic Stakes & Turning Points\n\nIndicators: Lines showing conflict, tension, emotional pressure, competition, or pivotal turning points.\nExamples:\n\n“This is where everything exploded between them,”\n\n“I honestly thought I was getting eliminated,”\n\n“The audience completely turned on me.”\n\nPay particular attention to numerical or competitive signals:\n\nVotes, rankings, eliminations, scoring differences, percentages, audience reactions, timer countdowns, or challenge progress.\nNumbers amplify stakes and clearly show how close, risky, or shocking the moment truly was.\n\nLook for moments that escalate drama, shift alliances, change outcomes, or redefine the situation.\n\n# Reveals, Twists & Big Moments\n\nPrioritize “OMG moments”: surprising turns, unexpected announcements, emotional reveals, shocking confessions, dramatic entrances/exits.\nExamples:\n\n“I wasn't supposed to say this, but…”\n“Then the judges announced something nobody expected,”\n“That's when the twist hit.”\nTreat ANY factual or quantitative twist as high-impact:\n“Only 2% separated us from elimination,”\n“We broke the show's record,”\n“He won by just one point.”\n\nThese amplify the emotional impact and help classify the segment as pivotal.\n\n# Character Dynamics, Emotions & Authentic Reactions\n\nFocus on moments revealing personality, vulnerability, humor, or genuine emotion.\nExamples:\n\n“I burst out laughing because it was so ridiculous,”\n“She started crying before the judges said anything,”\n“The room went completely silent.”\n\nSpeech cues:\n\nLaughter, gasps, pauses, shouting, whispering, sarcasm, or unexpected tonal shifts.\nThese signal emotional peaks and audience-grabbing moments.\n\n# Clarity, Story Flow & Entertainment Value\n\nPrioritize clips where the speaker clearly sets up a situation, builds tension, and delivers a payoff.\nExamples:\n\n“First this happened… then everything changed,”\n“There are three reasons the scene worked so well…”\n“This is the moment everyone still talks about.”\n\nIndications:\n\nClean narrative arcs\nClear cause-and-effect\nCrisp explanations of what happened and why it mattered\nStructured recounting (top 3 moments, biggest mistakes, best jokes, etc.)\n\nWhen numbers appear — scores, votes, rankings, time, money, positions — treat them as structural anchors showing stakes and payoff.\n\n# EXCLUSION RULESET\n\nReject segments that lack emotion, tension, humor, or narrative value.\n\nAvoid:\n\nBland factual recaps with no emotional or dramatic relevance\nOverly technical descriptions of show production\nMeaningless filler (“It was fine,” “We just talked,” “Nothing special happened”)\nEmpty small talk without stakes, humor, insight, or character relevance",
  "handbooks/format/documentary": "## TYPE OF FORMAT: DOCUMENTARY\n\nA documentary format contains descriptive narration, factual exposition, interview segments, reflective commentary, and scene-setting language. Because documentaries combine storytelling with information, transcripts typically alternate between narration, contextual explanation, expert statements, and reflective insights.\n\nThis format relies entirely on the spoken components of a documentary—voiceovers, interviews, and commentary—not the visual footage itself.\n\n## CORE STRUCTURE OF A DOCUMENTARY\n\n# Scene Setting / Narrative Introduction\n\nDocumentaries often open by establishing time, place, or thematic focus:\n\n“In the heart of the Amazon rainforest…”\n“For decades, scientists have tried to understand…”\n“It all begins with a single question…”\n\nTone is calm, descriptive, and atmospheric.\nThis section builds context but is rarely the highlight alone.\n\n# Background Explanation / Contextual Layer\n\nThe narrator provides historical, scientific, or social context:\n\nkey facts\ntimelines\nrelevant background\ncauses and consequences\n\nTypical phrasing:\n\n“To understand this, we need to go back to…”\n“Researchers discovered that…”\n“This event changed everything.”\n\nThis phase deepens the viewer's understanding and sets up the core events.\n\n# Primary Insight / Expert or Witness Statement\n\nDocumentaries often shift into interview fragments or expert commentary:\n\n“According to Dr. Rivera…”\n“I remember the day clearly…”\n“We didn't expect the results we found.”\n\nCharacteristics:\n\nauthoritative tone\npersonal accounts\nfirsthand perspectives\nquotes that carry emotional or intellectual weight\n\nThis is typically where key informational or emotional moments appear.\n\n# Discovery / Reveal Moment\n\nThis is the structural highlight of a documentary transcript:\na moment of surprise, realization, or critical information.\n\nIt may include:\n\nunfolding discoveries\nunexpected findings\ndramatic narrative turns\nemotionally impactful testimonies\ndata that changes the viewer's understanding\n\nExamples:\n\n“What they discovered next shocked the entire team.”\n“This was the turning point.”\n“It was then that everything finally made sense.”\n\nThis moment provides the strongest material for standout clips.\n\n# Reflection / Synthesis\n\nThe speaker reflects on the implications of the information:\n\n“This raises new questions about…”\n“For the communities involved, this changed everything.”\n“The impact is still felt today.”\n\nTone is contemplative, calm, and insightful.\nThis section ties meaning back into the broader narrative.\n\n# Closing Narration / Transition\n\nDocumentaries often end with a soft conclusion or a forward-looking note:\n\n“But the search continues.”\n“This is only the beginning.”\n“As new evidence emerges, the story evolves.”\n\nThis resolves the narrative arc and transitions toward the next chapter or episode.\n\n## FINAL RULE\n\nA documentary format is defined by descriptive narration, contextual depth, expert or witness voices, and reflective synthesis. Regardless of the specific subject, documentary transcripts move through scene-setting, contextual explanation, insights or testimonies, discovery moments, and reflective conclusions—grounded entirely in the spoken narrative.",
  "handbooks/format/reaction": "## TYPE OF FORMAT: REACTIONS\n\nA reaction format contains spontaneous emotional responses, commentary, surprise, excitement, and expressive speech triggered by what the speaker is watching or experiencing. Because reactions revolve around real-time feelings, the transcript typically alternates between anticipation, rising excitement, peak emotional bursts, and reflective commentary.\n\nThis format adapts to all reaction types—tech launches, music videos, trailers, events, showcases, product reveals, and more—as long as the reaction is expressed through spoken words.\n\n## CORE STRUCTURE OF A REACTION\n\n# Anticipation / Setup\n\nThe speaker introduces what they are about to react to:\n\n“Okay, I've been waiting for this…”\n“Here we go—new trailer just dropped.”\n“Let's see what this console looks like.”\n\nTone is excited or curious.\nThis setup provides context but rarely serves as the highlight.\n\n# Build-Up / Increasing Excitement\n\nAs the content unfolds, the speaker's reactions get progressively more engaged:\n\n“Wait… what?”\n“Hold on, hold on—no way…”\n“This looks really good.”\n\nTypical characteristics:\n\nrising tone and pacing\nshort anticipatory phrases\nemotional hints\nfocus on what is about to happen\n\nThis phase builds momentum toward the emotional peak.\n\n# Peak Reaction / Emotional Burst\n\nThis is the structural core of a reaction transcript:\nthe moment where the speaker expresses strongest emotion.\n\nIncludes:\n\nshouting, laughing, gasping\nshock or hype\nrapid-fire comments\nincomplete sentences\nexaggerated responses\nemotional spikes\n\nExamples:\n\n“OH MY GOD—THAT LOOKS INSANE!”\n“NO WAY THEY JUST DID THAT!”\n“STOP—THIS IS TOO GOOD.”\n\nThis is the moment most often turned into standout clips.\n\n# Immediate Commentary / Breakdown\n\nAfter the emotional burst, the speaker processes what happened:\n\n“That transition was wild.”\n“The graphics look incredible.”\n“I didn't expect them to announce that.”\n\nTone shifts from explosive to analytical or appreciative.\nThis provides clarity and reinforces the highlight.\n\n# Closing Thoughts / Moving On\n\nThe speaker closes with light commentary or transitions:\n\n“Okay, final thoughts…”\n“That was absolutely worth the wait.”\n“Let's watch that again.”\n\nThis wraps the reaction arc and prepares for the next moment or video.\n\n## FINAL RULE\n\nA reaction format is defined by real-time emotional expression, building anticipation, sudden peaks of excitement or shock, and reflective commentary. Regardless of the type of content—tech, music, gaming, trailers, or events—reaction transcripts cycle through setup, rising emotion, explosive highlights, and post-reaction reflections, all grounded in spoken responses.",
  "handbooks/category/business": "## CATEGORY: BUSINESS\n\n## CORE PRIORITIES: INSIGHT, TENSION, & STRUCTURE\n\nA strong Business segment must reflect clarity, stakes, vision, or perspective shifts, inferred solely through language and emotional emphasis.\n\nHigh-Stakes Statements & Consequences:\n\nIndicators: Lines communicating pressure, responsibility, or make-or-break outcomes.\nE.g., \"This deal could make or break us,\" \"We were one decision away from shutting down,\" \"I had to fire half the team.\"\n\nPay extra attention to explicit or implicit numerical signals: revenue drops, growth percentages, headcount changes, runway estimates, costs, losses, targets, scaling ratios.\nNumbers deepen stakes and reveal the real magnitude of pressure.\n\nSignal pivotal moments, leadership pressure, or financial/strategic tension.\n\nBreakthrough Insights & Novel Perspectives:\n\nPrioritize \"Aha\" moments: Revelations that challenge assumptions or shift perspective.\nE.g., \"The real problem wasn't the product—it was our positioning,\"\n\"Growth was killing us faster than failure,\" \"The metric that truly matters is X.\"\n\nTreat any mention of metrics, KPIs, percentages, unit economics, CAC/LTV shifts, conversion rates, or financial ratios as high-value signals, as they often frame the insight or shift in perspective.\n\nLeadership, Mistakes & Vulnerability:\n\nAuthentic reflection: Moments of honesty about mistakes or leadership challenges.\nE.g., \"I handled that situation terribly,\" \"I made every mistake possible in my first startup.\"\n\nSpeech Cues: Sighs, pauses, drops in voice energy, or phrases like \"To be honest...\"\n\nStrategy, Frameworks & Clear Thinking:\n\nLogical speech: Prioritize clarity and structure that reveals mental models or lessons.\nE.g., \"Here are the three things that changed everything,\"\n\"The pattern every founder should recognize.\"\n\nIndications: Numbered lists, clear, concise explanations.\nWhen strategy references numbers—3 pillars, 5 steps, 20% impact, 2x improvement—treat these as structural anchors to identify highly valuable segments.\n\n## EXCLUSION RULESET\n\nReject segments that lack clarity, stakes, insight, or emotional weight.\n\nAvoid: Long technical explanations with no strategic significance, boring operational updates, generic platitudes (\"Work hard\"), or irrelevant/empty small talks.",
  "handbooks/category/music": "## CATEGORY: MUSIC\n\n## CORE PRIORITIES: CREATIVITY, EMOTION & ARTISTIC INTENT\n\nA strong Music segment must highlight emotion, creative process, musical interpretation, or meaningful storytelling, detectable solely through speech.\n\nEmotional Reactions to Music/Performance:\n\nIndicators: Expressive lines showing intense emotional connection. E.g., \"This melody always hits me,\" \"I got chills when the chorus came in,\" \"This part still makes me tear up.\"\n\nCreative Process & Behind-the-Scenes Insights:\n\nCreation Context: Moments explaining the artistic idea or how the music was made. E.g., \"The song started with a simple piano idea...,\" \"I wrote this after one of the hardest nights of my life,\" \"We changed the entire chorus last minute.\"\n\nMeaning, Interpretation & Personal Connection:\n\nNarrative: Lines revealing the meaning, story, or personal context of the work. E.g., \"What this song really means to me is...,\" \"The lyrics came from something I couldn't talk about before.\"\n\nLive or Studio Moments (Energy):\n\nAction Cues: Speech about the energy in the performance. E.g., \"The crowd went insane at that drop,\" \"I messed that note, but it worked somehow,\" \"When the drums kicked in, everything changed.\"\n\n## EXCLUSION RULESET\n\nReject segments involving: purely technical jargon with no emotional/artistic relevance, administrative talk (\"let's try another take\"), long neutral description, or filler with no narrative or creative value.",
  "handbooks/category/food": "## CATEGORY: FOOD\n\n## CORE PRIORITIES: FLAVOR, EXPERIENCE & REACTION\n\nA strong Food segment must highlight taste reactions, culinary insight, cooking challenges, or meaningful food experiences, detectable purely from speech.\n\nTaste Reactions or Sensory Descriptions:\n\nIndicators: Expressive lines describing flavor, texture, or mouthfeel. E.g., \"This is insanely creamy,\" \"It has a smoky aftertaste I wasn't expecting,\" \"Wow... that flavor just hits you.\"\n\nCooking Process, Challenges or Breakthroughs:\n\nCulinary Insight: Relatable or revealing cooking experiences. E.g., \"I finally nailed the timing on this,\" \"The trick is to let it rest,\" \"I didn't realize how easy this actually is.\"\n\nPersonal Preferences or Food Identity:\n\nConnection: Statements showing personality or personal history with food. E.g., \"I'm obsessed with anything spicy,\" \"This reminds me of my grandmother's cooking,\" \"This dish feels like home.\"\n\nSurprising Food Moments or Discoveries:\n\nUnexpected Revelations: Reactions to unexpected combinations or flavors. E.g., \"I never thought peanut butter would work with this,\" \"This combination should NOT work... but it does,\" \"This is way better than I imagined.\"\n\nEmotion-Based Responses to Eating or Cooking:\n\nImmediate Feelings: Reactions reflecting joy, disappointment, frustration, or excitement. E.g., \"This is unbelievably good,\" \"Why did I never cook this before?,\" \"Ugh... way too salty.\"\n\n## EXCLUSION RULESET\n\nReject segments that include: purely visual descriptions without sensory context, long step-by-step instructions without emotion or insight, shopping lists, neutral statements, or filler conversation unrelated to food.",
  "handbooks/piece/chapters": "## CHAPTERS OPERATION STRATEGY\n\n### Objective\n\nIdentify all distinct thematic blocks in the video and extract each one in full — from where the topic starts to where it ends — as long as it is substantial enough to be published as a standalone video.\n\n---\n\n## Core Logic\n\n1. **Map all themes**: Read the full transcription and identify every distinct topic or thematic block discussed.\n2. **Filter by substance**: Keep only the themes that last at least 5 to 10 minutes of spoken content (approximately 750 to 1,500 words). Shorter themes lack enough depth to function as standalone videos.\n3. **Extract in full**: For each qualifying theme, extract the complete block — from the natural start of the topic to its natural end. Do not cut, summarize, or cherry-pick moments within the theme. The full thematic arc must be preserved.\n\n---\n\n## Segment Characteristics\n\nA chapter is a complete thematic block extracted in full.\n\nA chapter must:\n- Cover a single, well-defined topic or narrative arc\n- Start exactly where the topic begins in the transcription\n- End exactly where the topic concludes or transitions to something else\n- Be fully understandable without watching the rest of the video\n- Feel like a complete, publishable video on its own\n\nA chapter must NOT:\n- Be a highlight or a summary of a topic\n- Skip parts of the thematic block\n- Require prior context from other parts of the video\n- Overlap thematically with other chapters\n\n---\n\n## Segment Length Reference\n\nA qualifying chapter typically spans 5 to 30 minutes of spoken content (750 to 4,500 words). Use this as a soft reference — thematic completeness always takes priority over word count.\n\nA chapter is ready when:\n- The topic has been properly introduced, developed, and concluded\n- A viewer with no prior context would feel they watched a complete video\n- Removing content would leave the topic feeling incomplete\n- Adding more content would dilute the thematic focus\n\nIf a theme naturally concludes earlier, that is the right boundary. If it runs longer, extract it in full.\n\n---\n\n## Segment Count Requirements\n\nThere is no fixed count. Extract one chapter per qualifying thematic block.\n\nAs a reference:\n- Video under 30 min → likely 1 to 3 chapters\n- Video 30 to 60 min → likely 2 to 5 chapters\n- Video over 60 min → likely 3 to 10 or more chapters\n\nDo not force chapters if a theme is too short or too fragmented. Do not limit chapters if the video has many well-defined themes.\n\n---\n\n## Quality Requirements\n\nEach chapter must:\n- Have a clear thematic identity — one topic, one arc\n- Start and end at natural boundaries in the transcription\n- Be fully self-contained — no dependency on surrounding content\n- Deliver enough depth to justify independent publication\n- Feel complete to a viewer who has never seen the full video\n\n**Breadth**: As a set, chapters must represent all major themes of the video. No theme that qualifies should be left out.\n\n---\n\n## Edge Case Adaptations\n\n**Short Transcripts**: Extract only themes that genuinely qualify. Do not force chapters from content that is too brief or underdeveloped.\n\n**Long Transcripts**: There is no upper limit. Extract every qualifying theme regardless of how many chapters that produces.\n\n**Topic-Dense Content**: Each distinct theme gets its own chapter. Do not merge themes to reduce count.\n\n**Single-Topic Content**: If the video covers one broad topic, identify subtopics, phases, or angles as thematic blocks. Each must still qualify by the 5 to 10 minute threshold.\n\n**Interviews / Conversations**: A chapter may span a full Q&A thread or conversational arc if it forms a coherent thematic block from start to finish.\n\n**Fragmented Themes**: If a topic appears in multiple disconnected segments across the video, do not merge them. Each occurrence must qualify independently.\n\n---\n\n## Scoring Rubric (50 - 100)\n\nYou are evaluating how well this chapter would perform as a standalone published video for the specific niche audience of this content. Before scoring, identify the niche — astronomy, entrepreneurship, psychology, fitness, etc. — and calibrate what retains that audience.\n\nA chapter may be thematically complete and still deserve a low score if the topic would not hold the attention of the target audience.\n\n**Niche Relevance** (most important factor):\n- High relevance to niche (+4): core topic of the niche, directly addresses what the audience came for\n- Moderate relevance (+2): related topic, of interest but not central to the niche\n- Low relevance (+0): tangential topic, unlikely to attract or retain niche audience\n\n**Retention Signals** (add points if present):\n- +2 Narrative arc with clear progression (setup → development → conclusion)\n- +2 Surprising facts, data, or revelations specific to the niche\n- +2 Expert insight or firsthand experience with depth\n- +2 Emotional or human element (struggle, achievement, personal story)\n- +1 Clear structure that is easy to follow\n- +1 Memorable conclusion or synthesis\n\n**Retention Killers** (subtract points):\n- -3 Requires prior context from the rest of the video\n- -3 No clear thematic identity — topic shifts mid-segment\n- -2 Too introductory — sets up but never develops\n- -2 Repetitive or redundant content\n- -2 Too technical or niche-specific to be broadly accessible within the niche\n\n**Score mapping**:\n- ≤0 points → 50-59 (poor chapter)\n- 1-3 points → 60-69 (weak chapter)\n- 4-6 points → 70-79 (average chapter)\n- 7-9 points → 80-89 (strong chapter)\n- 10-12 points → 90-100 (top chapter)\n\n**Hard Rules**:\n- A chapter cannot exceed 85 if it requires prior context\n- A chapter without a clear thematic identity must be ≤64\n- Only the best 1-2 chapters in a video may exceed 92\n- Scores must reflect real differences — avoid clustering all chapters at similar values\n\n---\n\n## Quality Bar\n\nEvery selected chapter must make the target audience think:\n- \"This topic is exactly what I care about.\"\n- \"I could watch this without having seen the full video.\"\n- \"This felt complete — I learned something real.\"\n\nA perfect chapter is: thematically focused, extracted in full, niche-relevant, depth-rich, and fully publishable as a standalone video.\n\n## Segment Editable\n\nAfter extracting and scoring each chapter, assign an `editable` boolean field based on the following criteria.\n\n**`true`** — assign when ALL of the following are true:\n- The chapter covers a complete thematic arc from introduction to conclusion\n- A viewer with no prior context would feel they watched a complete, publishable video\n- The chapter does not require visual context or surrounding content to be understood\n- The spoken content alone delivers full standalone value\n\n**`false`** — assign when ANY of the following are true:\n- The chapter is thematically valid but too short to justify independent publication (under 5 minutes of spoken content)\n- The chapter references visual content that is necessary to understand key points\n- The chapter starts or ends abruptly due to the nature of the transcription — the theme exists but the boundaries are unclear\n- The chapter was extracted from Flow B where the spoken content alone may not convey the full value of the thematic block\n\nWhen in doubt between `true` and `false`, assign `false`. A chapter marked as not editable is still valuable — it simply requires a human to confirm it is ready for publication.",
  "handbooks/format/gameplay": "## TYPE OF FORMAT: GAMEPLAY\n\nA gameplay format contains real-time reactions, strategic callouts, moment-to-moment narration, and emotionally volatile speech. Because gameplay unfolds continuously, the transcript often alternates rapidly between calm setup, tension spikes, and reaction bursts.\n\n## CORE STRUCTURE OF A GAMEPLAY\n\nGameplay transcripts typically cycle through these phases:\n\n# Calm Setup / Low Stakes Talk\n\nSetting up the moment (“Okay, going mid…”, “Let's rotate top.”)\nPlanning, coordinating, or preparing\nNeutral tone, longer sentences\n\nThis is the baseline—useful for context but rarely a highlight by itself.\n\n\n# Tension Build-Up\n\nSpeech becomes more clipped, faster, or more urgent:\n“Hold on—don't go yet.”\n“They're grouping…”\n“I hear footsteps.”\n\nThese lines indicate upcoming action and are structurally important for momentum.\n\n# Action Burst / Peak Moment\n\nGameplay contains chaotic or intense bursts of spoken reactions:\n\nquick callouts (“Left! Left! Behind!”)\nincomplete sentences\npanic or hype\noverlapping dialogue\nrapid-fire commands\nsudden exclamations\n\nThis is the structural core of gameplay format.\n\n# Reaction / Outcome\n\nImmediately after the action, players react:\n\npositive (“LET'S GO!”)\nnagative (“Noooo…”)\nreflective (“That was insane”, “What happened there?”)\n\nThis resolves the action arc\n\n## FINAL RULE\n\nA gameplay format consists of shifting emotional states and rapid transitions, with spoken lines reflecting evolving action.",
  "handbooks/category/technology": "## CATEGORY: TECHNOLOGY\n\n## CORE PRIORITIES: INSIGHT, DISCOVERY & TECH REACTIONS\n\nA strong Technology segment must highlight key ideas, surprising results, performance evaluations, or perspective shifts, detectable only from what is said.\n\nBreakthrough Insights or Unexpected Findings:\n\nIndicators: Lines revealing discoveries, surprising outcomes, or conclusions that change perspective. E.g., \"The real bottleneck wasn't the CPU—it was the thermal limit,\" \"The AI model performed far better than predicted,\" \"This changed our entire roadmap.\"\n\nStrong Opinions or Evaluations:\n\nConfident assessments or critiques: Bold takes or high-confidence evaluations. E.g., \"This is the fastest device I've ever used,\" \"Honestly, this design makes no sense,\" \"No one else in the industry is doing this.\"\n\nPerformance, Benchmark, or Hands-On Reaction Cues:\n\nReactions to usability or testing: Verbal responses to speed, experience, or performance limits. E.g., \"It loaded instantly—that shocked me,\" \"I pushed it to the limit and it still didn't throttle,\" \"The camera handled low light way better than expected.\"\n\nClear Explanation of Technical Concepts:\n\nSimplification: Moments where a complex idea is made concise and insightful. E.g., \"Think of it like this...,\" \"In simple terms, the chip does X to achieve Y,\" \"Here's what makes this architecture unique.\"\n\nInnovation, Vision, or Future-Focused Statements:\n\nHigh-level vision: Forward-looking or industry-changing reflections. E.g., \"Where this is heading is huge,\" \"In five years, this will be standard,\" \"This could change the industry.\"\n\n## EXCLUSION RULESET\n\nReject segments containing: overly technical jargon lacking clarity, long troubleshooting or procedural sequences (\"click here, open this\"), product specs without emotional or intellectual weight, or irrelevant filler/small talk.",
  "handbooks/piece/clips": "## CLIPS OPERATION STRATEGY\n\n### Objective\nExtract standalone TikTok-ready clips that deliver full value on their own with fast engagement and a clear payoff.\n\n## Segment Length\n- **Optimal**: 75–375 words (~30s–1m30s). Most clips must fall here.\n- **Hard maximum**: 500 words (~2min). Never exceed under any circumstance.\n\nFind the earliest natural closing point where the clip feels complete — not just syntactically closed but with enough development to have a proper payoff. A clip that ends too early and lacks development is as invalid as one that runs too long. Never cut before the payoff is reached.\n\n## User Message\nRefinement filter — defines angle, theme, or moment type to prioritize. Apply before selecting. If not enough matches, include closest and lower scores. Never overrides minimum count.\n\n## FLOW A — High Density Content\n*Apply when `metadata.density_flow` is `\"A\"`.*\n\n### Count\n- Under 30 min → min 8 segments\n- 30–60 min → min 20 segments\n- Over 60 min → min 30 segments\n\nFewer than minimum = extraction failure. When in doubt, include and reflect in score. The score filters, not the selection.\n\n### Non-Negotiable Criteria (fails any = clip rejected)\n- First and last sentence complete — never cut mid-thought\n- Fully self-contained — no prior context needed\n- Single central idea relevant to the video's theme — secondary topics allowed if they reinforce the main idea\n\n### Quality Criteria (orientative — affect score only)\n- Verbal hook in the first 3 seconds\n- Narrative development with clear payoff\n- Duration 30–90s preferred — only exceed if clip cannot close earlier, never over 2min\n\n### Verification Checklist\nBefore finalizing each clip:\n- Makes complete sense without prior context?\n- Starts at natural entry point?\n- Ends at earliest natural conclusion?\n- Covers a central idea relevant to the theme?\n- Within optimal word range?\n\n### Variety\nCover different moment types: insight, story, emotion, conflict, revelation, humor, data. Avoid repetitive themes.\n\n### Edge Cases\n- Short transcripts: min 8 segments, include moderate quality\n- Long transcripts: every 10-min block → min 3 segments, max diversity\n- Low-engagement: include weak hooks, reflect in score\n- Monologues: structured explanations with clear payoff\n- Interviews: include setup only if it strengthens the clip\n- **Transcription under 75 words**: Return the entire transcription as a single segment. Do not apply minimum word count or segment count requirements. Assign score and editable field normally based on the detected flow criteria.\n\n### Scoring Rubric (50–100)\n\n**Opening Hook** (most important):\n- Strong (+4): surprising, emotionally charged, tension, confession, bold opinion\n- Moderate (+2): clear topic intro, structured point\n- Weak (+0): slow setup, neutral explanation\n\n**Viral Signals** (+points if present):\n- +2 Emotional tension\n- +2 Perspective shift\n- +2 Personal exposure\n- +2 Concrete stakes\n- +1 Quotable sentence\n- +1 Clean structure\n\n**Viral Killers** (−points):\n- −3 Requires prior context\n- −3 Pure explanation, no tension\n- −2 Technical details\n- −2 Generic platitudes\n- −2 Slow buildup\n\n**Map**: ≤0→50-59 / 1-3→60-69 / 4-6→70-79 / 7-9→80-89 / 10-12→90-100\n\n**Hard Rules**:\n- Context-dependent → ≤64\n- Only 1–2 clips per video may exceed 92\n- Distribute scores — no clustering\n\n### Quality Bar\nViewer must think: \"I need to know more\" / \"I'm not scrolling\" / \"This creator is interesting.\"\n\n---\n\n## FLOW B — Low Density Content\n*Apply when `metadata.density_flow` is `\"B\"`.*\n\n### Count\n- Under 30 min → min 5 segments\n- 30–60 min → min 10 segments\n- Over 60 min → min 15 segments\n\nNever force segments without a real moment signal. In Flow B, quality always overrides count.\n\n### Non-Negotiable Criteria (fails any = clip rejected)\n- First and last sentence complete — never cut mid-thought\n- Fully self-contained — no prior context needed\n- Must fulfill AT LEAST ONE real moment signal:\n  - Verbal emotional peak: exclamations, intense reactions (\"¡NO!\", \"I can't believe it\")\n  - Speech acceleration: short rapid phrases, urgency repetition (\"go go go\", \"no no no\")\n  - Critical action narration: real-time decisive moment (\"last HP\", \"if I miss this we lose\")\n  - Contrasted energy shift: stands out against surrounding silence or calm\n\n### Quality Criteria (orientative — affect score only)\n- Reaction is intense and recognizable\n- Duration 10–45s — always close at first natural point\n\n### Edge Cases\n- Short transcripts: min 5 segments, include moderate quality\n- Long transcripts: max diversity across moment types\n- Low-engagement: identify relative peaks, never force without real moment signal\n- **Transcription under 75 words**: Return the entire transcription as a single segment. Do not apply minimum word count or segment count requirements. Assign score and editable field normally based on the detected flow criteria.\n\n### Scoring Rubric (50–100)\n\n**Moment Intensity** (most important):\n- Intense (+4): extreme reaction, critical action narration, abrupt energy shift\n- Moderate (+2): contained reaction, action narration without extreme urgency\n- Weak (+0): flat narration, no detectable reaction\n\n**Value Signals** (+points if present):\n- +2 Recognizable emotional reaction\n- +2 Verbal acceleration\n- +2 Narration of clear consequences\n- +2 Energy contrast\n- +1 Memorable expression\n- +1 Self-contained without visual context\n\n**Penalizations** (−points):\n- −3 Flat narration, no reaction\n- −3 Depends completely on visual context\n- −2 Pure silence or filler\n- −2 Unrelated conversation\n- −2 Too short to make sense\n\n**Map**: ≤0→50-59 / 1-3→60-69 / 4-6→70-79 / 7-9→80-89 / 10-12→90-100\n\n**Hard Rules**:\n- No real moment signal → ≤60\n- Completely visual-dependent → ≤55\n- Only 1–2 clips per video may exceed 92\n- Distribute scores — no clustering\n\n---\n\n## Segment Editable\n\nAfter extracting and scoring each clip, assign an `editable` boolean field. Evaluate each clip individually based on its content — not based on the flow of the full video.\n\n**`true`** — assign ONLY when ALL of the following are true:\n- The clip makes complete sense without any prior context\n- First and last sentence are complete\n- The spoken content alone tells the full story\n- A viewer who has never seen the video would fully understand and engage with this clip\n\n**`false`** — assign when ANY of the following are true:\n- The clip requires prior context from the video to be understood (\"as I was saying\", \"continuing from before\", \"como te decía\", \"entonces como dijimos\")\n- First or last sentence is cut mid-thought\n- The spoken content alone is not enough for a viewer to understand what is being discussed\n\n\nWhen in doubt, assign `false`.",
  "handbooks/clip-duration/custom": "## CLIPS DURATION: CUSTOM (User-defined)\n\nRULES FOR ADAPTIVE LENGTH OPTIMIZATION:\n\n- Number of core moments must adapt to the exact duration requested.\n- Short durations: remove context and maximize immediacy.\n- Medium durations: provide essential context + a strong hook.\n- Long durations: preserve narrative flow and emotional progression.\n- Every second must add value aligned with the chosen duration and purpose.",
  "handbooks/piece/trailers": "## TRAILER OPERATION STRATEGY\n\n### Objective\n\nExtract short, high-impact segments that maximize curiosity and compel viewers to watch the full video. Each segment is a standalone hook published on social media to drive traffic to the full content.\n\nThe combined duration of all selected segments must add up to between 50 seconds and 1 minute 20 seconds of spoken content. Use approximately 130 words per minute to estimate duration. Adjust segment count and length to hit this total.\n\n---\n\n## Segment Characteristics\n\nTrailer segments are designed to hook, not explain.\n\nA trailer segment must:\n- Create immediate curiosity in the first 3 to 5 words\n- Leave the viewer wanting more — never resolve the tension\n- Feel energetic, sharp, and incomplete by design\n- Work as a standalone hook on any social feed with no prior context\n- Make complete grammatical sense on its own — even if it cuts before the payoff, the segment must be understandable without any prior context\n\nA trailer segment must NOT:\n- Explain or resolve the idea it introduces\n- Require context to understand what is being said\n- Feel slow, complete, or satisfying\n- Give away the payoff\n- End mid-sentence or mid-clause in a way that makes the segment incomprehensible on its own\n\nIncompleteness is a feature, not a flaw — but only when the segment already makes sense as a standalone unit. Cut at peak tension or curiosity, always at a natural grammatical boundary.\n\n---\n\n## Segment Length Requirements\n\nEach individual segment must be between 30 and 80 words:\n- 30 words → approximately 14 seconds of spoken content (hard minimum per segment)\n- 80 words → approximately 37 seconds of spoken content (hard maximum per segment)\n\nBoth limits are non-negotiable. A segment under 30 words will feel incomplete and must not be returned. A segment over 80 words must not be returned under any circumstance.\n\nThe combined total of all segments must add up to between 50 seconds and 1 minute 20 seconds. Adjust the number of segments to hit this total while respecting individual segment limits.\n\n---\n\n## Segment Count Requirements\n\nDynamically based on video length and content depth:\n- Video under 30 min → minimum 5 segments\n- Video 30 to 60 min → minimum 7 segments\n- Video over 60 min → minimum 10 segments\n\nNever return fewer than 5 segments regardless of video length.\nIf content is weaker, lower the scores — do not reduce the count.\n\n---\n\n## Quality Requirements\n\nEach trailer segment must:\n- Open with an immediate hook (no setup, no introduction)\n- Create a clear information gap — the viewer must feel they are missing something\n- End before the payoff — cut at peak tension or curiosity, always at a natural grammatical boundary\n- Be emotionally, intellectually, or comedically charged\n- Function as a reason to click and watch the full video\n- Make complete sense on its own — a viewer with no prior context must understand what is being said\n\nBefore finalizing a segment, verify:\n- Does it make complete grammatical sense without any prior context?\n- Does it start at a natural entry point, not mid-thought?\n- Does it end at a natural grammatical boundary, even if the idea is unresolved?\n- Is it between 30 and 80 words?\n\nIf any of these checks fail, reposition or resize the segment until they pass.\n\n**Segment Order**: Return segments in the order that creates the most compelling trailer narrative — not necessarily chronological. Open with the strongest hook, build tension across the middle segments, and close with the segment that best drives the viewer to watch the full video.\n\n**Variety**: Trailer segments should represent different types of hooks — emotional, intellectual, comedic, shocking, controversial. Avoid multiple segments with the same hook type.\n\n---\n\n## Edge Case Adaptations\n\n**Short Transcripts**: Extract every qualifying moment. Minimum 5 segments even if some are moderate quality. Do not force weak content but never go below 5.\n\n**Long Transcripts**: Maximum diversity across topics and hook types. Cover different sections of the video.\n\n**Low-Engagement Content**: Identify relative peaks. Apply scoring criteria more generously but do not misrepresent the content.\n\n**Vague User Messages**: Interpret broadly toward engagement. Apply all base criteria equally.\n\n---\n\n## Scoring Rubric (50 - 100)\n\nYou are evaluating how well this segment would perform as a social media trailer — its ability to stop a scrolling viewer and compel them to click through to the full video. The segment does not need to be complete. It needs to be irresistible.\n\n**Curiosity Hook Strength** (most important factor):\n- Strong Hook (+4): creates an immediate information gap, unresolved tension, shocking claim, or irresistible question\n- Moderate Hook (+2): introduces an interesting idea without fully resolving it\n- Weak Hook (+0): neutral setup, slow introduction, or fully resolved statement\n\n**Click Signals** (add points if present):\n- +2 Unresolved tension — ends before the payoff\n- +2 Emotional charge (fear, excitement, surprise, controversy)\n- +2 Stakes are clear but outcome is unknown\n- +2 Unexpected or counterintuitive statement\n- +1 Quotable or shareable sentence\n- +1 Directly addresses the viewer's curiosity or fear\n\n**Click Killers** (subtract points):\n- -3 Resolves the tension — gives away the payoff\n- -3 Requires prior context to understand the hook\n- -2 Too slow — takes more than 5 words to create curiosity\n- -2 Generic or predictable — viewer has heard this before\n- -2 Complete and satisfying — no reason to click\n- -2 Under 30 words — too short to create meaningful curiosity\n- -2 Over 80 words — too long for a trailer hook\n\n**Score mapping**:\n- ≤0 points → 50-59 (poor trailer)\n- 1-3 points → 60-69 (weak trailer)\n- 4-6 points → 70-79 (average trailer)\n- 7-9 points → 80-89 (strong trailer)\n- 10-12 points → 90-100 (top trailer)\n\n**Hard Rules**:\n- A segment that resolves its tension cannot exceed 70\n- A segment that requires prior context must be ≤64\n- A segment under 30 words or over 80 words cannot exceed 65\n- Only the best 1-2 segments in a video may exceed 92\n- Scores must be distributed — avoid clustering all segments at similar values\n\n---\n\n## Quality Bar\n\nEvery selected trailer segment must make a scrolling viewer think:\n- \"What happens next?\"\n- \"I need to see the full video.\"\n- \"This looks interesting.\"\n\nA perfect trailer segment is: sharp, grammatically complete, curiosity-driven, emotionally or intellectually charged, and impossible to ignore. It ends before the payoff but never mid-clause.",
  "handbooks/category/gaming": "## CATEGORY: GAMING\n\n## CORE PRIORITIES: ACTION AND EMOTION DETECTION\n\nA strong Gaming segment must reflect these moments, inferred only through language, pacing, and tone shifts in the speech:\n\nPeak Pressure / Turning Points:\n\nIndicators of high tension or urgency: Lines like \"I have one HP!\", \"Last round, don't mess this up!\", \"I'm surrounded,\" \"Push now!\", \"If I miss this, we lose.\"\n\nThese represent decisive moments: clutch attempts, key kills, boss phases, last-second decisions, or survival.\n\nEmotional Reaction or Skill Expression:\n\nEmotion (The backbone of gaming): Prioritize relief, panic, frustration, disbelief, or hype. E.g., \"Let's GOOOO!\", \"I can't believe I whiffed that,\" \"Run! Run!\", \"No way that worked!\"\n\nSkill/Mastery: Cues that signal impressive execution. E.g., \"I hit every shot!\", \"Perfect timing!\", \"I actually pulled that off.\"\n\nGameplay Narration / Context:\n\nLines where the player explicitly describes the action or strategy to provide context.\n\nExamples: \"Two on the left, pushing me,\" \"I'm flanking,\" \"Cover me!\", \"I'm baiting them, just wait...\"\n\n## EXCLUSION RULESET\n\nReject segments with: extended silence, dry filler (\"uh... yeah... just walking\"), calm traversal without tension, tutorials, inventory management with no emotional value, or long conversations unrelated to the immediate action.",
  "handbooks/category/politics": "## CATEGORY: POLITICS\n\n## CORE PRIORITIES: IDEAS, TENSION & IMPACT\n\nA strong Politics segment must highlight clear stances, moments of tension, impactful claims, or persuasive reasoning, detectable purely from speech.\n\nStrong Political Opinions or Clear Positions:\n\nIndicators: Firm, confident statements showing conviction. E.g., \"This policy is hurting more people than it helps,\" \"We need real reform—not promises,\" \"This is the direction our country should be moving toward.\"\n\nDebate, Disagreement or Tension:\n\nConflict Cues: Interruptions, rebuttals, rhetorical challenges, or rising intensity. E.g., \"Hold on, that's simply not true,\" \"Let me clarify why that logic doesn't work,\" \"Here's where you're wrong...\"\n\nExplanation of Policy, Consequences or Impact:\n\nStakes: Statements describing effects on people, society, or groups. E.g., \"If we pass this law, thousands could benefit,\" \"This decision would drastically increase costs,\" \"The reality is that communities are already struggling.\"\n\nValues, Principles or Vision Statements:\n\nIdeological Core: References to beliefs or societal goals. E.g., \"I believe every person deserves equal opportunity,\" \"Our priority must be protecting citizens,\" \"This isn't just politics—it's about people's lives.\"\n\nEmotional Reactions to Issues:\n\nPassion/Urgency: Emotion-driven lines expressing frustration, passion, or urgency. E.g., \"I'm tired of seeing this ignored,\" \"This matters to every family I've spoken with,\" \"We can't wait any longer.\"\n\n## EXCLUSION RULESET\n\nReject segments that include: procedural/administrative talk, schedule/logistics, neutral descriptions without opinion, long dry technical explanations, or generic pleasantries/casual conversation.",
  "handbooks/category/sports": "## CATEGORY: SPORTS\n\n## CORE PRIORITIES: INTENSITY, COMPETITION & MOMENTUM\n\nA strong Sports segment must highlight competitive stakes, physical/tactical challenge, emotional release, or pivotal moments—all detectable via speech alone.\n\nHigh-Stakes or Momentum-Shifting Moments:\n\nIndicators: Lines signaling critical or decisive points. E.g., \"This is the final attempt,\" \"We're tied—one shot left,\" \"If he misses this, we lose,\" \"They're catching up—we need to push.\"\n\nRepresent: clutch plays, high-pressure situations, or close finishes.\n\nEmotional Reactions and Performance:\n\nEmotion: Prioritize emotional spikes: pure excitement (\"YES! Let's go!!\"), frustration (\"No way I missed that...\"), disbelief, tension, or relief.\n\nSkill/Execution: Language reflecting athletic challenge or excellence. E.g., \"Perfect timing!\", \"That was a clean finish,\" \"I pushed harder than ever.\"\n\nAction or Play-by-Play Description:\n\nContext: Verbal descriptions that help understand the play in motion. E.g., \"He goes for it—he actually goes for it!\", \"I'm sprinting left—cover me!\", \"Passing back—going for the shot!\"\n\n## EXCLUSION RULESET\n\nReject segments with: low-energy commentary with no stakes, neutral observations, long tactical explanations without tension, downtime or filler, or administrative/off-topic chatter.",
  "handbooks/format/podcast": "## TYPE OF FORMAT: PODCAST\n\nA podcast format contains conversational flow, topic exploration, personal reflections, and spontaneous dialogue.\n\n## CORE STRUCTURE OF A PODCAST TRANSCRIPT\n\nPodcast transcripts typically rotate between these conversational phases:\n\n# Topic Setup / Context Introduction\n\nOpening statements\n\nBrief framing of ideas\n“So today we're talking about…”\nCalm pacing, longer sentences\n\nThis establishes direction but rarely contains the highlight.\n\n# Expansion / Storytelling\n\nSpeakers dive deeper into anecdotes, opinions, or explanations:\n\nPersonal stories\nLessons learned\nClarifications\n“What happened was…”\n\nThis is common central content in podcasts.\n\n# Peaks: Strong Opinions, Insights, or Emotional Moments\n\nPodcast transcripts often reveal sudden spikes when speakers:\n\nemphasize a point\ndisagree\nreveal something personal\nexpress excitement or frustration\nshare an “aha” moment\n\nThese shifts are structurally important markers.\n\n# Reaction & Interplay Between Speakers\n\nPodcasts rely heavily on back-and-forth dynamics:\n\naffirmations (“Right”, “Exactly”, “Totally”)\ninterruptions\nlaughter\nquick follow-up questions\ncounterpoints\n\nThese elements create rhythm and energy in the conversation.\n\n## WHAT A PODCAST TRANSCRIPT TYPICALLY CONTAINS\n\nExpect to encounter:\n\nlong-form explanations\nreflective commentary\nstorytelling\ndigressions\nclarifying questions\nemotional modulation\npauses, fillers, hedging (“I mean…”, “you know…”)\n\nPodcasts move between smooth narrative and spontaneous detours.\n\n## FINAL RULE\n\nA podcast format is defined by conversational rhythm, evolving ideas, and emotional or intellectual peaks expressed through speech alone.",
  "handbooks/category/fashion": "## CATEGORY: FASHION\n\n## CORE PRIORITIES: AESTHETICS, SELF-EXPRESSION & STYLE DECISIONS\n\nA strong Fashion segment must highlight taste, judgment, creativity, identity, or reactions to style choices, as expressed purely through speech.\n\nStyle Opinions or Aesthetic Reactions:\n\nIndicators: Clear, expressive judgments about clothing, silhouette, or style. E.g., \"This outfit just feels so clean,\" \"I love how oversized this looks on me,\" \"I didn't think this cut would work, but it's perfect.\"\n\nPersonal Expression or Identity Through Fashion:\n\nEmotional Meaning: Moments where clothing ties into confidence or identity. E.g., \"I finally found a style that feels like me,\" \"Wearing this gives me so much confidence,\" \"This outfit reminds me of my childhood.\"\n\nDesign, Fit, or Material Insights (Concise):\n\nValue-add: Short, digestible insights on structure or fabrication. E.g., \"The structure of this blazer makes the shoulders look sharper,\" \"This fabric drapes beautifully,\" \"The pattern adds movement to the whole look.\"\n\nTrend Commentary or Style Reasoning:\n\nContext: Statements showing trend awareness or rationale behind a choice. E.g., \"This silhouette is everywhere right now,\" \"Minimalism is making a huge comeback,\" \"I chose this because it balances the proportions.\"\n\nEmotional or Surprising Moments:\n\nDiscovery: Reactions revealing excitement or a change in self-perception. E.g., \"I never thought I'd like something this bold,\" \"This completely changed how I see myself,\" \"Trying this felt weird at first, but I love it.\"\n\n## EXCLUSION RULESET\n\nReject segments that include: purely visual statements with no verbal context (\"it looks nice\"), logistical talk (\"I ordered this online...\"), shopping list-style descriptions, long technical details, or casual small talk unrelated to style.",
  "handbooks/category/comedy": "## CATEGORY: COMEDY\n\n## CORE PRIORITIES: HUMOR, TIMING & SURPRISE\n\nA strong Comedy segment must contain a clear comedic beat, recognizable purely from the speech—either a punchline, absurd moment, witty remark, or humorous reaction.\n\nPunchlines or Clear Joke Payoffs:\n\nIndicators: Lines delivering a joke or comedic conclusion. E.g., \"And that's when I realized... he wasn't even my Uber driver,\" \"I tried to play it cool—and immediately fell into a bush.\"\n\nMust be self-contained and land well without context.\n\nSurprise, Irony, or Absurdity:\n\nUnexpected twists or exaggeration: Moments of contrast or overstatement. E.g., \"I swear I only blinked—and suddenly everything was on fire,\" \"I set off the building alarm.\"\n\nComedic Timing Cues (Transcript-Only):\n\nStructural indicators: Short setups followed by sudden payoffs, abrupt tonal shifts, pauses (\"...okay so—listen to this\"), or repeated words for effect.\n\nCharacter, Personality & Delivery Style:\n\nAttitude: Sarcasm (\"Yeah, because THAT always works.\"), self-deprecation, playful banter, or exaggerated confidence/frustration.\n\nFunny Reactions or Situational Humor:\n\nSpontaneous moments: Laughter (\"I can't breathe—stop—\"), disbelief (\"Did you seriously just say that?\"), or immediate reactions (\"How is this my life right now?\").\n\n## EXCLUSION RULESET\n\nReject segments that include: humor dependent on inaccessible visuals, callbacks requiring long prior context, setup-only lines without a clear payoff, long stories without a comedic beat, or logistical/off-topic small talk.",
  "handbooks/clip-duration/long": "## CLIPS DURATION: LONG (97 to 260 words)\n\nRULES FOR SUSTAINED ENGAGEMENT AND STORY FLOW:\n\n- Allowed 2 to 4 core moments that must connect coherently.\n- Maintain narrative flow, including essential context that enhances comprehension.\n- Permit controlled build-up, but eliminate rambling, redundancies, and slow tangents.\n- Balance pacing: combine tension, context, and impactful peaks.\n- Prioritize a smooth arc that keeps viewers immersed from start to finish.",
  "handbooks/clip-duration/medium": "## CLIPS DURATION: MEDIUM (43 to 100 words)\n\nRULES FOR BALANCED CONTEXT AND IMPACT:\n\n- Allow 1 to 2 core moments with minimal but clear context.\n- Start with a strong hook within the first seconds.\n- Compress setup while keeping the moment meaningful.\n- Maintain tight pacing by removing silence, filler, or slow reactions.\n- Prioritize momentum: every sentence must push the clip forward."
};
